welcome to data vis deaf talk I'm your  host - cozy our today's guest is Mark  Soares mark is the manager of analytic  solution at click insight mark is also a  constant advocate for effective data  visualization and storytelling welcome  to the show thanks - it's good to be  here  so tell audience a little bit about  yourself what is your role at click  insight I am manager of our analytics  solutions team at click insight we are a  digital analytics consultancy we're also  a Google marketing platform partner and  my team is responsible for everything  from implementing analytics on a website  setting up Google tag manager creating  reports and dashboards often with Google  Data studio all the way to conducting  analysis and providing insights and  recommendations to our clients so you  could say we do the full stack of  digital analytics so you do a lot of  visualizations with data studio how do  you get started with it I was interested  in the area from the very beginning I  started using it about as early as it  was available and I think there were a  couple key benefits that I saw from very  early on that attracted me to the tool  one was the native integration with  Google Analytics the other main benefit  was the ease of sharing reports you can  share a link and the viewer can have  access to that report even without  logging in or downloading another  application and so for those two reasons  I was really attracted to data studio in  the first place and what's kept me  interested is all the updates and  enhancements that have been rolled out  over time to really make it a more  powerful reporting product so you  started with Google Analytics data and  you've been creating reports that you  share with your team members and your  clients but recently you've been using  videos for you for other purposes as  well for example you make almost  weekly contributions to the maker monday  contest so how do you get started with  that the whole practice of taking this  new data set every week and then  creating visualizations from that data  set makeover Monday is a community  project where every week there's a new  data set and visual posted with the idea  being that participants will take that  same data set and create an improved  version of the chart I started  participating in the group Monday even  before I started using Google data  studio and it was for the reason of  giving myself an opportunity to practice  and improve my data visualization skills  and by having that different chart a  different topic different type of data  set every week it provides a great  opportunity to practice and learn new  skills and with data studio by forcing  yourself to be in the tool regularly I  can keep my skills up to date and learn  some of the new features and  enhancements in the tool what if your  visualizations were also including the  makeover Monday book what is your  process for creating these  visualizations like how do you start  with the you said what is your process  for picking chart types or forms and  colors and like would it be the size of  everything where do you start how do you  go through the process and how do you  publish I think I have established a  pretty consistent process over the time  that I've been doing maker one day it  always starts with the data the data  sets usually provided in a spreadsheet  or CSV format when I'm using data studio  the first thing I do is create a data  source so that I have access to those  fields in data studio but before I jump  into creating any charts or visuals I  like to get to know the data set a bit  more I take a look at what fields are  available what dimensions and metrics  are in the data set and that often  requires reading some background  material and where that data comes from  often it's part of a larger report or it  was published as part of an article or  there's some more description about that  data set I think that's always important  to understand the data before you start  using it  to make sure that whatever  interpretations are going to make or  however you're going to tell the story  that it's consistent with what the data  actually represents once I get to know  the data I can go into creating some  charts in data studio I try to choose  the most appropriate chart for the  message that I would like to communicate  or the story that I want to tell  depending on whether I'm trying to show  all the data or focus on a particular  aspect of it I may make different  decisions about which charts are most  appropriate that often involves some  experimentation in the tool but once  I've decided on what the most  appropriate way is to visualize the data  I can then add to it with more visual  aspects of colors and fonts and layout  to really make a more polished final  product but I think that overall is the  process that I follow every week with  maker one day it is interesting that  when you're talking about the  visualization it's not just the chart  types you also have the data so you have  to have some domain knowledge about the  data then there's a storytelling part  like what story in the write what story  am I going to tell with this whole data  and then building it is based on that on  those two principles your approach to  data visualization seems to be very  unique you're using the common chart  types like bar charts and scatter plots  but then you're operating within the  same techniques and limitations but you  are coming up with something very unique  what inspires you like how do you get  inspiration for these unique approaches  to data visualization yeah I think I  draw inspiration from a lot of different  sources with many of the makeover Monday  visualizations that I've created I do  try to push the boundaries of what's  possible with the tool in particular the  data studio and try to show something  that perhaps people haven't seen before  or they didn't think was possible with  the tool but at the same time I always  try to respect the data and the story  that I'm trying to tell and make sure  that the message is clear  and then the visual doesn't get in the  way of that and in that context I have  drawn inspiration from people like  Edward Tufte and Stephen few who have  written a lot about the field of data  visualization and the principles of  communicating clearly with information I  also draw inspiration from others that  contribute to the visualization  community there are a lot of creative  and talented people that create and  share data visualizations and I think I  try and pull bits and pieces from  everything that I see and apply that in  my own work talking about community  you've built a number of community  visualizations and community connectors  for data studio  this committee is still young and  growing when you think about the  developer community and the prospects  that a new developer has in building  solutions with their studio I've done a  bunch of work with the community  connectors and visualizations creating  those custom components for a data  studio one thing I should clarify is  that I don't consider myself a developer  I can certainly code and I'm comfortable  with JavaScript and HTML and CSS but I'm  not a full-time developer nor have I  ever been a full-time developer but I  think that speaks to the ease with which  the custom components for data studio  can be created it doesn't require an  expert developer to be able to connect  to data sources or create custom  visualizations for data studio you do  need a bit of programming experience and  knowledge but I think what's more  important is to understand the structure  of the data how it would be used in a  report or dashboard and putting that  knowledge together I think gives anyone  a really good ability to create these  custom components right now the data vis  community around data studio is still  growing but I think because of the  framework that's in place it provides a  great opportunity for more developers to  get involved  and build have more custom components  would it be your favorite tip that you'd  give to our audience with data studio  that's a good question  I think or at least one of my favorite  tips is something that I do in my  makeover Monday visualizations very  frequently it's a rather simple thing  but I hide the page boundary what it  does is creates a very clean and crisp  look to your visualization the  background fills the screen and if you  go into full-screen mode then you really  have a true full screen experience so  it's something very simple but I think  it has a great impact on the final  visualization when would you use a pie  chart I think I may only use a pie chart  to explain to you why you shouldn't use  buy charts they're certainly not my  favorite chart type and there are many  reasons why other chart types are most  often better alright folks that's the  end of our show thanks again to mark for  being here if you want to see more  content on data studio make sure that  you subscribe to the GCP Channel and you  can always visit our developer site at  developers.google.com until the next  time keep making more visualizations  [Applause]
 

